  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how a factor linked to inflammation affects the heart and surrounding cardiovascular tissues. The left side emphasizes immune activation, showing macrophages and fibroblasts as key targets. In macrophages, inflammasome activation and cathepsin activity appear to drive release of inflammatory mediators such as TNF, IL‑1β, and IL‑18. Fibroblasts are shown producing IL‑8, and the text also indicates altered lymphocyte responses, including effects on T helper cells and shifts in CD4+ versus CD8+ cell accumulation after myocardial infarction.\n\nThe center places these mechanisms in the context of the whole heart, while the right side highlights major pathological consequences. One outcome is valve calcification, illustrated as increased calcification in valvular smooth muscle cells. Another is left ventricular hypertrophy, with thicker ventricular walls and molecular signs of hypertrophy and stress. The figure also links the inflammatory state to myocardial dysfunction through fibrosis and cell death: fibroblasts show increased TGFβ, pSMAD2, and pSMAD3 signaling, leading to myofibroblast differentiation and production of collagen, fibronectin, αSMA, and ED‑A fibronectin. Overall, the image presents inflammation as a driver of calcification, hypertrophy, fibrosis, and cardiomyocyte apoptosis in the heart.",
